WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.240
(upbeat music)

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.912
- [Narrator] Monkeys definitely
feed off of your feelings.

00:00:04.912 --> 00:00:06.392
They know when you're feeling down,

00:00:06.392 --> 00:00:08.091
or when you're feeling funny.

00:00:08.091 --> 00:00:09.261
It's kind of an urban legend

00:00:09.261 --> 00:00:11.251
that people out there are training monkeys

00:00:11.251 --> 00:00:15.800
to help people with disabilities,
but that really happens.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.300
- Nice going, too.

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:21.709
I have my monkey, Casey,

00:00:21.709 --> 00:00:23.377
for 10 years.

00:00:23.377 --> 00:00:26.127
In 2005, I was in a car accident.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.033
I injured C1, C2, the spinal cord,

00:00:32.757 --> 00:00:34.424
with a brain injury.

00:00:36.538 --> 00:00:38.517
I couldn't move

00:00:38.517 --> 00:00:40.837
really anything at all.

00:00:40.837 --> 00:00:42.549
I couldn't talk.

00:00:42.549 --> 00:00:45.299
You know, I've come a long way.

00:00:45.299 --> 00:00:47.819
- Our goal is to train
and place capuchin monkeys

00:00:47.819 --> 00:00:51.318
with people living with
mobility impairments.

00:00:51.318 --> 00:00:53.839
The program got its start
really as an experiment,

00:00:53.839 --> 00:00:55.789
to say, we can train a dog

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:57.949
to help somebody who
has visual impairment,

00:00:57.949 --> 00:01:00.011
perhaps we can train a
primate to be the hands

00:01:00.011 --> 00:01:02.701
for somebody who has had
a spinal cord injury.

00:01:02.701 --> 00:01:05.891
We provide our monkeys at
no charge to our recipients.

00:01:05.891 --> 00:01:06.974
- Good fetch!

00:01:08.723 --> 00:01:10.941
- [Megan] All of our monkeys
are born into our program.

00:01:10.941 --> 00:01:13.083
Typically a monkey will spend
about three to five years

00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:14.512
at the monkey college,

00:01:14.512 --> 00:01:17.032
learning tasks, whether
that's fetching a phone

00:01:17.032 --> 00:01:19.323
or a remote control off the floor.

00:01:19.323 --> 00:01:22.222
- Atta girl, Daisy, atta girl.

00:01:22.222 --> 00:01:23.736
- [Megan] Helping somebody
get something to drink.

00:01:23.736 --> 00:01:25.535
- Good job, girl.
- Even something

00:01:25.535 --> 00:01:29.215
as complicated as flipping
the pages of a book.

00:01:29.215 --> 00:01:31.275
The monkeys do really act as hands

00:01:31.275 --> 00:01:35.504
and give them that level
of independence back.

00:01:35.504 --> 00:01:37.285
You can imagine having
a spinal cord injury

00:01:37.285 --> 00:01:38.685
and all of a sudden you have a monkey

00:01:38.685 --> 00:01:39.518
that's sitting on your shoulder

00:01:39.518 --> 00:01:41.516
and you can finally feel that touch.

00:01:41.516 --> 00:01:43.196
To have that monkey there

00:01:43.196 --> 00:01:46.373
is an incredible bonding experience.

00:01:46.373 --> 00:01:48.476
- When I couldn't move

00:01:48.476 --> 00:01:50.647
Casey did a lot for me.

00:01:50.647 --> 00:01:51.730
Casey, brush.

00:01:55.116 --> 00:01:58.116
Now it's a lot of emotional support.

00:02:00.847 --> 00:02:03.180
It's a lot of companionship.

00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:08.525
Casey is part of my family.

00:02:08.525 --> 00:02:11.495
You know, this is a five-pound animal

00:02:11.495 --> 00:02:14.078
that is extraordinarily helpful

00:02:14.984 --> 00:02:17.151
and brings joy to my life.

00:02:18.165 --> 00:02:20.748
(upbeat music)

00:02:23.926 --> 00:02:26.843
(electronic music)

